{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01N47RyE1urxwteWR3aBqDyX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Public ABI (matches `nm -D` of the C shared object):\n4\t//!   * `extractFilename`\n5\t//!   * `FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir`\n6\t//!\n7\t//! The behaviour of the C original is reproduced exactly, including its quirks\n8\t//! (e.g. the out-of-bounds `outDirName[strlen(outDirName)-1]` read when\n9\t//! `outDirName` is the empty string, and the `exit(30)` on allocation failure),\n10\t//! and including the fatal signal it raises on a NULL argument — the C performs\n11\t//! no null checks whatsoever, so neither does this translation.\n12\t\n13\t#![allow(non_snake_case)]\n14\t\n15\tuse core::ffi::{c_char, c_int, c_void};\n16\t\n17\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n18\t// libc bindings\n19\t//\n20\t// The buffer returned by FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir() is expected to be\n21\t// released by the caller with free(), therefore it must come from the very same\n22\t// allocator the C code used: libc's calloc().\n23\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n24\tunsafe extern \"C\" {\n25\t    fn calloc(nmemb: usize, size: usize) -> *mut c_void;\n26\t    fn exit(status: c_int) -> !;\n27\t    fn fprintf(stream: *mut c_void, format: *const c_char, ...) -> c_int;\n28\t    fn strerror(errnum: c_int) -> *mut c_char;\n29\t    fn __errno_location() -> *mut c_int;\n30\t\n31\t    // The C source calls these libc routines directly, so the translation binds\n32\t    // to the very same implementations rather than re-implementing them. This\n33\t    // matters for more than just speed: a hand-written Rust `strlen` triggers\n34\t    // rustc's \"null pointer dereference occurred\" check, which turns into a\n35\t    // non-unwinding panic (SIGABRT) inside an `extern \"C\"` function, whereas the\n36\t    // C faults inside glibc with SIGSEGV. Binding to libc reproduces the C's\n37\t    // fatal-signal behaviour exactly, under every build profile.\n38\t    fn strlen(s: *const c_char) -> usize;\n39\t    fn strrchr(s: *const c_char, c: c_int) -> *mut c_char;\n40\t    fn memcpy(dst: *mut c_void, src: *const c_void, n: usize) -> *mut c_void;\n41\t\n42\t    #[allow(non_upper_case_globals)]\n43\t    static mut stderr: *mut c_void;\n44\t}\n45\t\n46\t/// Platform path separator, mirroring the `#if defined(_MSC_VER) ||\n47\t/// defined(__MINGW32__) || defined(__MSVCRT__)` block of the C source.\n48\t#[cfg(windows)]\n49\tconst SEPARATOR: c_char = b'\\\\' as c_char;\n50\t#[cfg(not(windows))]\n51\tconst SEPARATOR: c_char = b'/' as c_char;\n52\t\n53\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n54\t// Public ABI\n55\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n56\t\n57\t/// ```c\n58\t/// const char* extractFilename(const char* path, char separator)\n59\t/// ```\n60\t///\n61\t/// Returns the portion of `path` following the last occurrence of `separator`,\n62\t/// or `path` itself when the separator does not occur.\n63\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n64\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn extractFilename(path: *const c_char, separator: c_char) -> *const c_char {\n65\t    // `strrchr(path, separator)`: the C `char` argument undergoes the usual\n66\t    // integer promotion to `int`, i.e. it is SIGN-extended (`char` is signed on\n67\t    // this target). `separator as c_int` reproduces that promotion exactly.\n68\t    // SAFETY: `path` is a NUL terminated C string, as required by the C API.\n69\t    let search: *const c_char = unsafe { strrchr(path, separator as c_int) };\n70\t    if search.is_null() {\n71\t        return path;\n72\t    }\n73\t    // SAFETY: `search` points inside `path`, so one-past it is in bounds.\n74\t    unsafe { search.add(1) }\n75\t}\n76\t\n77\t/// ```c\n78\t/// char* FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir(const char* path,\n79\t///                                     const char* outDirName,\n80\t///                                     const size_t suffixLen)\n81\t/// ```\n82\t///\n83\t/// Takes a source file name and specified output directory, and allocates\n84\t/// memory for and returns a pointer to the final path. This function never\n85\t/// returns an error (it may `exit(30)` in case of a problem).\n86\t///\n87\t/// The returned buffer is obtained from libc `calloc()` and must be released by\n88\t/// the caller with `free()`.\n89\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n90\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir(\n91\t    path: *const c_char,\n92\t    outDirName: *const c_char,\n93\t    suffixLen: usize,\n94\t) -> *mut c_char {\n95\t    let separator: c_char = SEPARATOR;\n96\t\n97\t    // SAFETY: both pointers are NUL terminated C strings, as required by the C\n98\t    // API; all pointer arithmetic below mirrors the original C exactly.\n99\t    unsafe {\n100\t        let mut filenameStart: *const c_char = extractFilename(path, separator);\n101\t        if cfg!(windows) {\n102\t            /* sometimes, '/' separator is also used on Windows (mingw+msys2) */\n103\t            filenameStart = extractFilename(filenameStart, b'/' as c_char);\n104\t        }\n105\t\n106\t        let outDirLen = strlen(outDirName);\n107\t        let filenameLen = strlen(filenameStart);\n108\t\n109\t        // calloc(1, strlen(outDirName) + 1 + strlen(filenameStart) + suffixLen + 1)\n110\t        // Wrapping arithmetic reproduces the C size_t overflow behaviour.\n111\t        let size = outDirLen\n112\t            .wrapping_add(1)\n113\t            .wrapping_add(filenameLen)\n114\t            .wrapping_add(suffixLen)\n115\t            .wrapping_add(1);\n116\t        let result = calloc(1, size) as *mut c_char;\n117\t        if result.is_null() {\n118\t            fprintf(\n119\t                stderr,\n120\t                c\"zstd: FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir: %s\".as_ptr(),\n121\t                strerror(*__errno_location()),\n122\t            );\n123\t            exit(30);\n124\t        }\n125\t\n126\t        memcpy(result as *mut c_void, outDirName as *const c_void, outDirLen);\n127\t        // NOTE: for an empty `outDirName` the C code reads `outDirName[-1]`;\n128\t        // that out-of-bounds access is preserved verbatim here.\n129\t        if *outDirName.wrapping_add(outDirLen.wrapping_sub(1)) == separator {\n130\t            memcpy(\n131\t                result.add(outDirLen) as *mut c_void,\n132\t                filenameStart as *const c_void,\n133\t                filenameLen,\n134\t            );\n135\t        } else {\n136\t            memcpy(\n137\t                result.add(outDirLen) as *mut c_void,\n138\t                &separator as *const c_char as *const c_void,\n139\t                1,\n140\t            );\n141\t            memcpy(\n142\t                result.add(outDirLen).add(1) as *mut c_void,\n143\t                filenameStart as *const c_void,\n144\t                filenameLen,\n145\t            );\n146\t        }\n147\t\n148\t        result\n149\t    }\n150\t}\n151\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cda4f09f-3588-4dea-80fb-419403d7c77f","uuid":"8779f0d0-e6d0-483f-870c-48a1824217d3","timestamp":"2026-08-27T15:56:45.589Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-DE4CkO/translation/src/lib.rs","content":"//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n//!\n//! Public ABI (matches `nm -D` of the C shared object):\n//!   * `extractFilename`\n//!   * `FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir`\n//!\n//! The behaviour of the C original is reproduced exactly, including its quirks\n//! (e.g. the out-of-bounds `outDirName[strlen(outDirName)-1]` read when\n//! `outDirName` is the empty string, and the `exit(30)` on allocation failure),\n//! and including the fatal signal it raises on a NULL argument — the C performs\n//! no null checks whatsoever, so neither does this translation.\n\n#![allow(non_snake_case)]\n\nuse core::ffi::{c_char, c_int, c_void};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// libc bindings\n//\n// The buffer returned by FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir() is expected to be\n// released by the caller with free(), therefore it must come from the very same\n// allocator the C code used: libc's calloc().\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nunsafe extern \"C\" {\n    fn calloc(nmemb: usize, size: usize) -> *mut c_void;\n    fn exit(status: c_int) -> !;\n    fn fprintf(stream: *mut c_void, format: *const c_char, ...) -> c_int;\n    fn strerror(errnum: c_int) -> *mut c_char;\n    fn __errno_location() -> *mut c_int;\n\n    // The C source calls these libc routines directly, so the translation binds\n    // to the very same implementations rather than re-implementing them. This\n    // matters for more than just speed: a hand-written Rust `strlen` triggers\n    // rustc's \"null pointer dereference occurred\" check, which turns into a\n    // non-unwinding panic (SIGABRT) inside an `extern \"C\"` function, whereas the\n    // C faults inside glibc with SIGSEGV. Binding to libc reproduces the C's\n    // fatal-signal behaviour exactly, under every build profile.\n    fn strlen(s: *const c_char) -> usize;\n    fn strrchr(s: *const c_char, c: c_int) -> *mut c_char;\n    fn memcpy(dst: *mut c_void, src: *const c_void, n: usize) -> *mut c_void;\n\n    #[allow(non_upper_case_globals)]\n    static mut stderr: *mut c_void;\n}\n\n/// Platform path separator, mirroring the `#if defined(_MSC_VER) ||\n/// defined(__MINGW32__) || defined(__MSVCRT__)` block of the C source.\n#[cfg(windows)]\nconst SEPARATOR: c_char = b'\\\\' as c_char;\n#[cfg(not(windows))]\nconst SEPARATOR: c_char = b'/' as c_char;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Public ABI\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n/// ```c\n/// const char* extractFilename(const char* path, char separator)\n/// ```\n///\n/// Returns the portion of `path` following the last occurrence of `separator`,\n/// or `path` itself when the separator does not occur.\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn extractFilename(path: *const c_char, separator: c_char) -> *const c_char {\n    // `strrchr(path, separator)`: the C `char` argument undergoes the usual\n    // integer promotion to `int`, i.e. it is SIGN-extended (`char` is signed on\n    // this target). `separator as c_int` reproduces that promotion exactly.\n    // SAFETY: `path` is a NUL terminated C string, as required by the C API.\n    let search: *const c_char = unsafe { strrchr(path, separator as c_int) };\n    if search.is_null() {\n        return path;\n    }\n    // SAFETY: `search` points inside `path`, so one-past it is in bounds.\n    unsafe { search.add(1) }\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// char* FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir(const char* path,\n///                                     const char* outDirName,\n///                                     const size_t suffixLen)\n/// ```\n///\n/// Takes a source file name and specified output directory, and allocates\n/// memory for and returns a pointer to the final path. This function never\n/// returns an error (it may `exit(30)` in case of a problem).\n///\n/// The returned buffer is obtained from libc `calloc()` and must be released by\n/// the caller with `free()`.\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir(\n    path: *const c_char,\n    outDirName: *const c_char,\n    suffixLen: usize,\n) -> *mut c_char {\n    let separator: c_char = SEPARATOR;\n\n    // SAFETY: both pointers are NUL terminated C strings, as required by the C\n    // API; all pointer arithmetic below mirrors the original C exactly.\n    unsafe {\n        let mut filenameStart: *const c_char = extractFilename(path, separator);\n        if cfg!(windows) {\n            /* sometimes, '/' separator is also used on Windows (mingw+msys2) */\n            filenameStart = extractFilename(filenameStart, b'/' as c_char);\n        }\n\n        let outDirLen = strlen(outDirName);\n        let filenameLen = strlen(filenameStart);\n\n        // calloc(1, strlen(outDirName) + 1 + strlen(filenameStart) + suffixLen + 1)\n        // Wrapping arithmetic reproduces the C size_t overflow behaviour.\n        let size = outDirLen\n            .wrapping_add(1)\n            .wrapping_add(filenameLen)\n            .wrapping_add(suffixLen)\n            .wrapping_add(1);\n        let result = calloc(1, size) as *mut c_char;\n        if result.is_null() {\n            fprintf(\n                stderr,\n                c\"zstd: FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir: %s\".as_ptr(),\n                strerror(*__errno_location()),\n            );\n            exit(30);\n        }\n\n        memcpy(result as *mut c_void, outDirName as *const c_void, outDirLen);\n        // NOTE: for an empty `outDirName` the C code reads `outDirName[-1]`;\n        // that out-of-bounds access is preserved verbatim here.\n        if *outDirName.wrapping_add(outDirLen.wrapping_sub(1)) == separator {\n            memcpy(\n                result.add(outDirLen) as *mut c_void,\n                filenameStart as *const c_void,\n                filenameLen,\n            );\n        } else {\n            memcpy(\n                result.add(outDirLen) as *mut c_void,\n                &separator as *const c_char as *const c_void,\n                1,\n            );\n            memcpy(\n                result.add(outDirLen).add(1) as *mut c_void,\n                filenameStart as *const c_void,\n                filenameLen,\n            );\n        }\n\n        result\n    }\n}\n","numLines":151,"startLine":1,"totalLines":151}}}
