{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_arhw63y52gmf2xto44e7l2yy3r2xiuopf7milstypzyqw42mkyhq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases passed, and I found and fixed a real bug.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/src/lib.c` (53 lines) exports two symbols — zstd's path-joining helpers:\n- `extractFilename(path, separator)` — returns the tail after the last separator\n- `FIO_createFilename_fromOutDir(path, outDirName, suffixLen)` — `calloc`s and builds `outDir + '/' + filename`\n\n## The bug I found and fixed\n\n**Phase C caught a genuine divergence on NULL input:** the C died with **SIGSEGV (11)**, the Rust with **SIGABRT (6)**.\n\nRoot cause: the translation had *re-implemented* `strlen`/`strrchr`/`memcpy` in Rust. The hand-written `strlen` did `*s.add(len)`, which tripped rustc's debug null-deref check; a panic crossing an `extern \"C\"` boundary is *non-unwinding*, so it `abort()`ed instead of faulting in glibc like the C does.\n\nFix: bind `strlen`, `strrchr`, `memcpy` to glibc — the exact routines the C source calls. This is also strictly more faithful (`strrchr`'s \"NUL is part of the string\" rule, and the C `char`→`int` sign-extending promotion, are now glibc's own semantics rather than a re-derivation).\n\nNotably, **the release build masked this bug entirely** — debug assertions are off there. That's why my harness runs both profiles.\n\n## I also validated the harness itself\n\nMatching symbols and green tests aren't sufficient, so I mutation-tested the Rust. One mutant **survived**: dropping `suffixLen` from the `calloc` size. My byte comparison read past the short allocation and found zeros, so it passed — a real hole. I fixed it with a `malloc_usable_size()` lower-bound assertion plus `0xAA` heap poisoning before each call. All 10 mutants are now killed; the 2 survivors are provably equivalent (sign- vs zero-extension is identical because C specifies `strrchr` compares `c` *converted to `char`*, and the `cfg!(windows)` branch is unreachable on this target).\n\nI also had to discard a `malloc_usable_size` *equality* assertion I initially wrote — glibc may serve a request from a larger bin, so the two libraries legitimately report different usable sizes (request 55 → 56 vs 72). The `>=` check is deterministic and is what actually catches under-allocation; I confirmed stability over 5 runs.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- **SYMBOLS.md** — `nm -D` diff is **empty**; zero undefined non-libc symbols (`ldd -r` clean). No module was skipped and nothing was stubbed.\n- **CONFIGS.md** — 28/28 rows pass, 0 unchecked. Drives the low-level `extractFilename` directly (not just the `lib.h` wrapper), plus composed and cross-linked-interior-pointer rows.\n- **ERRORS.md** — 12/12 rows pass, 0 unchecked, each mapped to a named test. Covers NULLs, `exit(30)` with stderr-text comparison, `size_t` wraparound, the unguarded `outDirName[-1]` OOB read (both branches, deterministically), and out-of-range narrow values passed as full `int`s across FFI.\n- **All of the above under every combination** — 2 feature combos × 2 profiles, all green, automated in `check_feature_combos.sh`.\n\nTwo caveats worth stating plainly: the Windows `#if defined(_MSC_VER)` branch is unreachable in both implementations on this Linux target, so it's documented rather than tested — it would need a Windows toolchain. And I deliberately did not test the `suffixLen` values that make *both* implementations overflow the heap by 1+ bytes; I tested only the exactly-fitting `SIZE_MAX` case, since the others corrupt the test process's heap identically in C and Rust without proving anything.\n\nEverything in `c_src/` is unmodified (only the instructed `build/` directory was added)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":680,"cache_read_input_tokens":136307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":680,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cda4f09f-3588-4dea-80fb-419403d7c77f","uuid":"8cb118cd-12bb-4cd2-9b6c-269585ecf9c2","timestamp":"2026-08-27T15:58:23.663Z"}
